# Local Engineering — SwarmForge VC (VS Code Extension)

Specializes the shared Engineering Rules for this project. Additive only; the
shared article remains in force.

## Tech Stack
- Language: TypeScript.
- Platform: Visual Studio Code extension (standard VS Code Extension API). Must
  also work in Cursor (a VS Code fork) — target the standard API only; nothing
  Cursor-specific.
- Standard scaffold (yo code / generator-code): normal package.json manifest,
  activation events, contributed commands.
- UI tiles render in a VS Code Webview.
- Node.js APIs (child_process, etc.) are available in the extension host for
  shelling out to tmux and git.
- Target OS: macOS and Linux only. Do not write Windows-specific code; the swarm
  substrate depends on tmux.

## Conventions
- Extension-host code and webview code stay clearly separated: different
  execution contexts, communicating only via postMessage. Never share state
  directly.
- The extension host owns all I/O: tmux, `.swarmforge/` files, git. The webview
  is presentation plus forwarded user input.
- Prefer small, testable modules (see shared Design And Testability — the
  webview and VS Code API surface are the unsuitable boundary; for Bubble,
  the Android device surface — see "Testability Boundary — Bubble" below).
- Command IDs namespaced under `swarmforge.` (swarmforge.run, swarmforge.stop,
  swarmforge.setTarget, swarmforge.initTarget).

## Architecture Rules
1. Two layers: tile-as-view, tmux-as-substrate — layers, not alternatives.
   Do NOT spawn agent processes directly from TypeScript to bypass tmux — if
   a task starts to feel like rebuilding process orchestration, stop; that
   is the signal the line was crossed.
2. Swarm machinery relationship. `swarmforge/` is a **maintained fork** of
   unclebob/swarm-forge, not an unmodified dependency — upstream changes
   enter only through drift-watch review, never automatic fetch. The
   bootstrap wrapper fetches helper scripts from a pinned by-SHA tarball,
   not a floating branch head.
3. Webview storage restriction. No localStorage, sessionStorage, or any browser
   storage in the webview. UI state lives in memory or persists via the
   extension host (workspace state / files).
4. Secrets. Tokens and keys (e.g. a GitHub token for opening PRs) live in the
   extension host environment only. Never written into the target working
   directory or any commit.
5. Two phone-viewable surfaces, one boundary. TWO web surfaces are
   phone-viewable and NOT interchangeable: (a) the STATIC backlog-dashboard
   PWA, a pure `./backlog.json` renderer with NO bridge/host connectivity,
   NO auth, NO write path; and (b) the LIVE holistic UI (token auth, live
   endpoints, control actions). Only git-SHA-derivable data rides the static
   PWA; route any live/machine-local/write-path need to the LIVE holistic UI
   instead — confirm which surface a request means before speccing.
   (Recurred 3x in 2026-07-10: BL-252 suite-duration trend, BL-265
   gate-answer view, BL-257 slices 3-4 changed-since-visit + live trends.)
   See **local-engineering-detailed.prompt** for the full pre-trim wording.
6. Epics have icons representing musical forms, on TWO distinct surfaces —
   never one implementation assumed to cover both: PWA/fleet-console/
   bot-avatar carry the owned orchestra FORM marks (`docs/branding/icon-system.md`
   §4d); Telegram topic icons stand in with the closest stock PERFORMANCE
   sticker (`getForumTopicIconStickers` offers no notation glyphs). See
   **local-engineering-detailed.prompt** for the full pre-trim wording and
   BL-449 rationale.
7. Interface vs incarnation — two naming layers, deliberately. Architecture
   names the INTERFACES (**messaging**, **host agent**); everyday speech
   names the live INCARNATIONS (**Telegram**, **Cursor**). The phone app is
   **Bubble** (native Android under `android/`; BL-707) — do not introduce a
   parallel brand in new prose. A proposal to rename identifiers/files to
   erase Telegram/Cursor "for purity" is OUT OF POLICY absent a real second
   incarnation AND a shim plan (human directive 2026-07-30). See
   **local-engineering-detailed.prompt** for the full doc-style guidance and
   the intake this rule came from.

## Testability Boundary — Bubble (Android) (BL-769)
Unsuitable boundary also includes the **Android device surface**. **Pure
logic** (no `android.*` type in the signature) → plain-JUnit JVM suite
(`./gradlew :app:testDebugUnitTest`, no emulator). **Device-surface
behavior** → a recorded manual procedure in the ticket; never ship a Bubble
feature file with no runnable binding. Robolectric/`androidTest` deliberately
not adopted. See **local-engineering-detailed.prompt** for the full rule
text. How-to: `docs/how-to/BL-769-android-jvm-unit-suite.md`.

## Diagrams (this project)
Per the shared Diagrams rule: (1) an **architecture diagram** (extension
host, webview(s), tmux substrate, `.swarmforge/` state, handoffd/queue
helpers, and the channels between them), and (2) a **swarm workflow flow
diagram** (the current parcel flow, updated in the same parcel as any
topology change). Both live under `docs/diagrams/` as Mermaid sources. See
**local-engineering-detailed.prompt** for the full pre-trim wording.

## Key Integration Surface
Prove these against a real swarm install, in order, before UI polish: launch
via `./swarm`; discover the tmux socket and enumerate sessions/windows/panes;
tail a pane live into a webview tile; send keystrokes INTO a pane (fiddliest
cross-platform piece — tackle early); read `.swarmforge/` state for the
holding stage; open a PR via git + GitHub CLI/API. Anything undoable from
outside the swarm machinery is a candidate fork change (normal backlog), not
an ad-hoc workaround. See **local-engineering-detailed.prompt** for the full
pre-trim wording.
